{"module":"columbus","voice":"male","duration":419.2,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Columbus: what his own records say","start":0.0,"end":2.86,"words":[[0.04,0.8],[1.1,1.35],[1.35,1.61],[1.61,1.86],[1.86,2.37],[2.37,2.62]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Columbus is usually taught as a discoverer.","start":3.66,"end":6.77,"words":[[3.7,4.31],[4.31,4.46],[4.46,5.0],[5.0,5.23],[5.23,5.38],[5.38,5.53],[5.53,6.3]]},{"el":"intro","text":"His own journal and letters, and the reports of the Spaniards who investigated him, tell a different story.","start":7.12,"end":14.07,"words":[[7.17,7.38],[7.38,7.6],[7.6,8.03],[8.03,8.24],[8.24,8.67],[8.92,9.13],[9.13,9.28],[9.28,9.71],[9.71,9.85],[9.85,9.99],[9.99,10.64],[10.64,10.78],[10.78,11.71],[11.71,12.0],[12.25,12.46],[12.46,12.6],[12.6,13.11],[13.11,13.53]]},{"el":"intro","text":"They also contain intriguing passages about Africans and the Atlantic, which have been both ignored and exaggerated.","start":14.42,"end":21.86,"words":[[14.47,14.68],[14.68,15.03],[15.03,15.52],[15.52,16.08],[16.08,16.64],[16.64,16.99],[16.99,17.56],[17.56,17.77],[17.77,17.91],[17.91,18.54],[18.78,19.06],[19.06,19.27],[19.27,19.48],[19.48,19.76],[19.76,20.11],[20.11,20.32],[20.32,21.31]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Here is what the records actually say.","start":22.21,"end":24.81,"words":[[22.25,22.47],[22.47,22.62],[22.62,22.84],[22.84,22.99],[22.99,23.43],[23.43,24.02],[24.02,24.31]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Who he was, and what he already knew","start":25.16,"end":28.07,"words":[[25.2,25.39],[25.39,25.58],[25.58,25.96],[26.29,26.57],[26.57,26.85],[26.85,27.04],[27.04,27.61],[27.61,27.89]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Columbus was a Genoese sailor who sailed for the Crown of Castile in 1492 under a contract, the Capitulations of Santa Fe, that promised him a tenth of the wealth he found and the titles of admiral and governor.","start":28.77,"end":43.91,"words":[[28.82,29.47],[29.47,29.71],[29.71,29.88],[29.88,30.45],[30.45,30.85],[30.85,31.02],[31.02,31.42],[31.42,31.59],[31.59,31.75],[31.75,32.16],[32.16,32.32],[32.32,32.89],[32.89,33.05],[33.05,34.11],[34.11,34.44],[34.44,34.6],[34.6,35.33],[35.62,35.78],[35.78,36.92],[36.92,37.08],[37.08,37.49],[37.49,37.74],[38.02,38.27],[38.27,38.84],[38.84,39.08],[39.08,39.24],[39.24,39.57],[39.57,39.73],[39.73,39.89],[39.89,40.22],[40.22,40.38],[40.38,40.79],[40.79,41.04],[41.04,41.2],[41.2,41.77],[41.77,41.93],[41.93,42.5],[42.5,42.75],[42.75,43.32]]},{"el":"s0","text":"He was looking for a western route to Asia.","start":44.26,"end":47.18,"words":[[44.3,44.45],[44.45,44.69],[44.69,45.08],[45.08,45.24],[45.24,45.4],[45.4,45.87],[45.87,46.11],[46.11,46.26],[46.26,46.66]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Before that, he had sailed with the Portuguese to their fortress at São Jorge da Mina (Elmina) on the coast of present-day Ghana, the center of Portugal's trade in gold and enslaved Africans.","start":47.53,"end":60.01,"words":[[47.58,47.9],[47.9,48.21],[48.49,48.64],[48.64,48.88],[48.88,49.27],[49.27,49.51],[49.51,49.66],[49.66,50.29],[50.29,50.45],[50.45,50.68],[50.68,51.16],[51.16,51.31],[51.31,51.55],[51.55,51.94],[51.94,52.1],[52.1,52.41],[52.41,52.88],[53.16,53.32],[53.32,53.47],[53.47,53.86],[53.86,54.02],[54.02,54.81],[54.81,55.2],[55.47,55.63],[55.63,56.02],[56.02,56.18],[56.18,56.89],[56.89,57.28],[57.28,57.44],[57.44,57.83],[57.83,58.07],[58.07,58.69],[58.69,59.4]]},{"el":"s0","text":"He knew that trade firsthand.","start":60.36,"end":62.41,"words":[[60.4,60.54],[60.54,60.74],[60.74,60.95],[60.95,61.29],[61.29,61.91]]},{"el":"s0","text":"His journal of the first voyage survives only as a summary made decades later by the friar Bartolomé de las Casas, partly quoting him and partly paraphrasing.","start":62.76,"end":73.39,"words":[[62.81,63.04],[63.04,63.5],[63.5,63.65],[63.65,63.81],[63.81,64.12],[64.12,64.58],[64.58,65.12],[65.12,65.51],[65.51,65.66],[65.66,65.81],[65.81,66.28],[66.28,66.59],[66.59,67.13],[67.13,67.51],[67.51,67.74],[67.74,67.9],[67.9,68.28],[68.28,68.98],[68.98,69.13],[69.13,69.36],[69.36,69.83],[70.1,70.48],[70.48,71.02],[71.02,71.25],[71.25,71.49],[71.49,71.87],[71.87,72.8]]},{"el":"h1","text":"What he wrote about the people he met","start":74.29,"end":76.44,"words":[[74.33,74.56],[74.56,74.71],[74.71,75.0],[75.0,75.38],[75.38,75.53],[75.53,75.9],[75.9,76.05],[76.05,76.28]]},{"el":"s1","text":"On October 13, 1492, the journal describes the Lucayan (Taíno) people as “none of them black, but the color of the Canary Islanders.”","start":77.14,"end":86.4,"words":[[77.19,77.33],[77.33,77.82],[77.82,78.24],[78.49,79.47],[79.71,79.85],[79.85,80.28],[80.28,80.91],[80.91,81.05],[81.05,81.54],[81.54,81.96],[82.2,82.56],[82.56,82.7],[82.7,82.91],[82.91,83.05],[83.05,83.26],[83.26,83.61],[83.85,84.06],[84.06,84.2],[84.2,84.48],[84.48,84.62],[84.62,84.76],[84.76,85.26],[85.26,85.89]]},{"el":"s1","text":"On October 14 it says: “with fifty men they could all be subjugated and made to do whatever one might wish.”","start":86.75,"end":92.79,"words":[[86.8,86.93],[86.93,87.38],[87.38,87.71],[87.71,87.84],[87.84,88.09],[88.32,88.51],[88.51,88.84],[88.84,89.03],[89.03,89.22],[89.22,89.42],[89.42,89.55],[89.55,89.68],[89.68,90.45],[90.45,90.64],[90.64,90.9],[90.9,91.03],[91.03,91.16],[91.16,91.55],[91.55,91.74],[91.74,92.0],[92.0,92.26]]},{"el":"s1","text":"From the first days, he wrote about them as future servants and converts.","start":93.14,"end":98.23,"words":[[93.15,93.46],[93.46,93.61],[93.61,93.91],[93.91,94.3],[94.56,94.72],[94.72,95.02],[95.02,95.4],[95.4,95.63],[95.63,95.79],[95.79,96.24],[96.24,96.78],[96.78,97.01],[97.01,97.62]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Enslavement and tribute on Hispaniola","start":99.13,"end":101.92,"words":[[99.18,100.05],[100.05,100.29],[100.29,100.85],[100.85,101.0],[101.0,101.88]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1495 Columbus's men captured about 1,500 Taíno; around 500 were shipped to Spain to be sold, and about 200 died on the voyage (Michele da Cuneo's eyewitness account).","start":102.62,"end":117.98,"words":[[102.67,102.84],[102.84,104.09],[104.09,104.92],[104.92,105.16],[105.16,105.75],[105.75,106.16],[106.16,107.91],[107.91,108.49],[108.78,109.28],[109.28,110.19],[110.19,110.35],[110.35,110.69],[110.69,110.85],[110.85,111.27],[111.27,111.43],[111.43,111.6],[111.6,112.1],[112.39,112.64],[112.64,113.05],[113.05,113.8],[113.8,114.13],[114.13,114.3],[114.3,114.46],[114.46,114.96],[114.96,115.38],[115.38,115.54],[115.54,116.21],[116.21,116.87],[116.87,117.45]]},{"el":"s2","text":"He imposed a tribute: every Taíno over 14 had to deliver a set amount of gold every three months.","start":118.33,"end":125.73,"words":[[118.38,118.55],[118.55,119.12],[119.12,119.28],[119.28,119.93],[120.22,120.54],[120.54,121.03],[121.03,121.36],[121.36,121.77],[121.77,122.01],[122.01,122.17],[122.17,122.66],[122.66,122.83],[122.83,123.07],[123.07,123.56],[123.56,123.72],[123.72,124.13],[124.13,124.46],[124.46,124.7],[124.7,125.19]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Las Casas, who lived on the island, wrote that those who failed were brutally punished, including having their hands cut off.","start":126.08,"end":133.61,"words":[[126.13,126.34],[126.34,126.77],[127.02,127.16],[127.16,127.45],[127.45,127.59],[127.59,127.73],[127.73,128.23],[128.48,128.77],[128.77,128.98],[128.98,129.26],[129.26,129.41],[129.41,129.76],[129.76,129.91],[129.91,130.4],[130.4,130.9],[131.15,131.72],[131.72,132.08],[132.08,132.29],[132.29,132.65],[132.65,132.86],[132.86,133.08]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2006 historians published the report of Francisco de Bobadilla, sent by the Crown in 1500 to investigate; witnesses described Columbus and his brothers ordering mutilations, whippings and executions.","start":133.96,"end":147.87,"words":[[134.01,134.15],[134.15,135.04],[135.04,135.73],[135.73,136.21],[136.21,136.35],[136.35,136.69],[136.69,136.83],[136.83,137.52],[137.52,137.65],[137.65,138.34],[138.58,138.85],[138.85,139.06],[139.06,139.2],[139.2,139.54],[139.54,139.68],[139.68,140.57],[140.57,140.71],[140.71,141.53],[141.77,142.32],[142.32,142.94],[142.94,143.49],[143.49,143.7],[143.7,143.9],[143.9,144.31],[144.31,144.73],[144.73,145.62],[145.86,146.27],[146.27,146.48],[146.48,147.3]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Bobadilla sent Columbus back to Spain in chains.","start":148.22,"end":151.6,"words":[[148.27,148.91],[148.91,149.19],[149.19,149.76],[149.76,149.97],[149.97,150.11],[150.11,150.47],[150.47,150.61],[150.61,151.1]]},{"el":"s2","text":"He was released but never again allowed to govern.","start":151.95,"end":155.02,"words":[[151.99,152.13],[152.13,152.35],[152.35,152.77],[152.77,152.99],[152.99,153.27],[153.27,153.56],[153.56,153.91],[153.91,154.05],[154.05,154.48]]},{"el":"h3","text":"How many died?","start":155.92,"end":157.17,"words":[[155.95,156.13],[156.13,156.38],[156.38,156.69]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Estimates of Hispaniola's population in 1492 range from about 100,000 to several million, and historians disagree sharply.","start":157.87,"end":167.74,"words":[[157.92,158.51],[158.51,158.66],[158.66,159.55],[159.55,160.37],[160.37,160.52],[160.52,161.49],[161.49,161.93],[161.93,162.23],[162.23,162.6],[162.6,163.86],[163.86,164.01],[164.01,164.46],[164.46,164.98],[165.24,165.46],[165.46,166.2],[166.2,166.72],[166.72,167.17]]},{"el":"s3","text":"A Spanish count in 1514 found only about 26,000 Taíno under forced labor; smallpox arrived in 1518.","start":168.09,"end":177.81,"words":[[168.14,168.31],[168.31,168.81],[168.81,169.22],[169.22,169.39],[169.39,170.31],[170.31,170.73],[170.73,171.14],[171.14,171.56],[171.56,172.98],[172.98,173.48],[173.48,173.82],[173.82,174.15],[174.15,174.65],[174.94,175.61],[175.61,176.11],[176.11,176.28],[176.28,177.28]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The collapse from violence, forced labor, famine and disease is documented; the starting number is debated.","start":178.16,"end":184.6,"words":[[178.21,178.33],[178.33,178.72],[178.72,178.97],[178.97,179.55],[179.77,180.03],[180.03,180.41],[180.64,180.96],[180.96,181.15],[181.15,181.47],[181.47,181.6],[181.6,182.36],[182.59,182.72],[182.72,183.1],[183.1,183.42],[183.42,183.55],[183.55,184.12]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Taíno did not vanish: genetic studies find substantial Taíno ancestry in Puerto Ricans and others today, and Taíno communities are reviving their culture.","start":184.95,"end":195.55,"words":[[185.0,185.15],[185.15,185.59],[185.59,185.81],[185.81,186.03],[186.03,186.47],[186.72,187.31],[187.31,187.75],[187.75,188.12],[188.12,188.85],[188.85,189.29],[189.29,189.88],[189.88,190.03],[190.03,190.54],[190.54,190.98],[190.98,191.2],[191.2,191.49],[191.49,191.93],[192.19,192.41],[192.41,192.85],[192.85,193.59],[193.59,193.66],[193.66,194.25],[194.25,194.47],[194.47,194.98]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Africans sailing west: what the records actually say","start":196.45,"end":200.12,"words":[[196.49,197.11],[197.11,197.57],[197.57,197.95],[198.22,198.45],[198.45,198.6],[198.6,199.07],[199.07,199.68],[199.68,199.91]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Several real documents raise the question of whether Africans crossed the Atlantic before 1492:","start":200.82,"end":207.73,"words":[[200.86,201.34],[201.34,201.65],[201.65,202.45],[202.45,202.77],[202.77,202.93],[202.93,203.56],[203.56,203.72],[203.72,204.04],[204.04,204.68],[204.68,205.08],[205.08,205.24],[205.24,205.87],[205.87,206.19],[206.19,207.3]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Columbus, third voyage (1498): in Las Casas's summary, Columbus says he sailed southwest partly to test King João II of Portugal's belief that there was land to the southwest, and that “canoes had been found which set out from the coast of Guinea [West Africa] and sailed to the west with merchandise.”","start":208.08,"end":228.73,"words":[[208.13,208.84],[209.12,209.36],[209.36,209.83],[209.83,211.1],[211.38,211.54],[211.54,211.78],[211.78,212.33],[212.33,212.89],[213.17,213.8],[213.8,214.04],[214.04,214.2],[214.2,214.59],[214.59,215.23],[215.23,215.63],[215.63,215.78],[215.78,216.1],[216.1,216.34],[216.34,216.82],[216.82,217.45],[217.45,217.61],[217.61,218.32],[218.32,218.72],[218.72,218.96],[218.96,219.2],[219.2,219.43],[219.43,219.75],[219.75,219.91],[219.91,220.07],[220.07,220.78],[221.06,221.3],[221.3,221.54],[221.54,221.93],[221.93,222.17],[222.17,222.41],[222.41,222.81],[222.81,223.12],[223.12,223.36],[223.36,223.6],[223.6,223.92],[223.92,224.08],[224.08,224.47],[224.47,224.63],[224.63,224.95],[224.95,225.27],[225.27,225.74],[225.74,225.98],[225.98,226.38],[226.38,226.54],[226.54,226.7],[226.7,227.01],[227.01,227.25],[227.25,228.12]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Reports of “black people” and guanín: the same accounts report that people on Hispaniola spoke of Black traders who had come from the south and southeast with spear points of guanín, a gold-copper alloy; guanín was also a Taíno word and material.","start":229.08,"end":245.57,"words":[[229.13,229.61],[229.61,229.77],[229.77,230.08],[230.08,230.48],[230.48,230.72],[230.72,231.28],[231.55,231.71],[231.71,232.03],[232.03,232.59],[232.59,232.99],[232.99,233.22],[233.22,233.62],[233.62,233.78],[233.78,234.65],[234.65,235.05],[235.05,235.21],[235.21,235.53],[235.53,236.08],[236.08,236.24],[236.24,236.48],[236.48,236.72],[236.72,237.04],[237.04,237.2],[237.2,237.52],[237.52,237.75],[237.75,238.31],[238.31,238.55],[238.55,238.87],[238.87,239.34],[239.34,239.5],[239.5,240.06],[240.34,240.5],[240.5,241.21],[241.21,241.61],[241.89,242.36],[242.36,242.6],[242.6,243.0],[243.0,243.16],[243.16,243.64],[243.64,243.87],[243.87,244.11],[244.11,244.99]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Panama, 1513: chronicler Peter Martyr wrote that Balboa's expedition met Black people at Quarequá, whom he guessed had come by sea from “Ethiopia.”","start":245.92,"end":255.7,"words":[[245.97,246.47],[246.73,247.6],[247.85,248.43],[248.43,248.72],[248.72,249.01],[249.01,249.3],[249.3,249.52],[249.52,250.1],[250.1,250.83],[250.83,251.04],[251.04,251.33],[251.33,251.7],[251.7,251.84],[251.84,252.42],[252.68,252.89],[252.89,253.04],[253.04,253.33],[253.33,253.55],[253.55,253.76],[253.76,253.98],[253.98,254.13],[254.13,254.42],[254.42,255.14]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Mali, about 1311: the scholar al-ʿUmarī recorded Mansa Musa saying that his predecessor (usually called Abu Bakr II) sent fleets into the Atlantic and then sailed himself, never to return.","start":256.05,"end":272.34,"words":[[256.13,256.55],[256.84,257.26],[257.26,258.25],[258.54,258.71],[258.71,259.13],[259.13,261.37],[261.37,261.95],[261.95,262.37],[262.37,262.78],[262.78,263.2],[263.2,263.45],[263.45,263.7],[263.7,264.44],[264.44,265.02],[265.02,265.36],[265.36,265.61],[265.61,265.94],[265.94,266.6],[266.89,267.23],[267.23,267.64],[267.64,267.97],[267.97,268.14],[268.14,268.81],[268.81,269.05],[269.05,269.3],[269.3,269.72],[269.72,270.38],[270.68,271.01],[271.01,271.17],[271.17,271.67]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Historian Ivan Van Sertima's They Came Before Columbus (1976) built on these to argue Africans reached the Americas.","start":272.69,"end":281.97,"words":[[272.73,273.48],[273.48,273.9],[273.9,274.15],[274.15,274.73],[274.73,274.98],[274.98,275.31],[275.31,275.65],[275.65,276.31],[276.31,277.81],[278.1,278.43],[278.43,278.6],[278.6,278.85],[278.85,279.02],[279.02,279.35],[279.35,280.01],[280.01,280.43],[280.43,280.6],[280.6,281.35]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Most archaeologists and historians conclude the case is unproven: no African settlement, artifact or pre-1492 African ancestry has been found in the Americas, and claims that the Olmec stone heads show Africans are rejected by Mesoamerican specialists and many Indigenous scholars, who see them as taking credit from Native peoples.","start":282.32,"end":304.38,"words":[[282.37,282.74],[282.74,283.7],[283.7,283.93],[283.93,284.67],[284.67,285.19],[285.19,285.34],[285.34,285.63],[285.63,285.78],[285.78,286.45],[286.71,286.93],[286.93,287.45],[287.45,288.19],[288.45,288.97],[288.97,289.04],[289.04,290.23],[290.23,290.75],[290.75,291.34],[291.34,291.56],[291.56,291.79],[291.79,292.16],[292.16,292.31],[292.31,292.45],[292.45,293.12],[293.38,293.6],[293.6,294.05],[294.05,294.27],[294.27,294.42],[294.42,294.79],[294.79,295.16],[295.16,295.46],[295.46,295.68],[295.68,296.27],[296.27,296.35],[296.35,297.01],[297.01,297.24],[297.24,298.2],[298.2,298.94],[298.94,299.16],[299.16,299.46],[299.46,300.2],[300.2,300.72],[300.98,301.13],[301.13,301.28],[301.28,301.5],[301.5,301.65],[301.65,302.09],[302.09,302.54],[302.54,302.84],[302.84,303.28],[303.28,303.8]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The documents are real; what they prove is debated.","start":304.73,"end":308.68,"words":[[304.78,304.94],[304.94,305.72],[305.72,305.8],[305.8,306.19],[306.47,306.7],[306.7,306.94],[306.94,307.25],[307.25,307.41],[307.41,308.12]]},{"el":"h5","text":"“African villages were already here when Columbus arrived”","start":309.58,"end":313.29,"words":[[309.63,310.19],[310.19,310.84],[310.84,311.0],[311.0,311.48],[311.48,311.73],[311.73,311.97],[311.97,312.61],[312.61,313.1]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Columbus's journal and letters don't describe African villages.","start":313.99,"end":318.53,"words":[[314.04,314.78],[314.78,315.22],[315.22,315.44],[315.44,315.81],[315.81,316.18],[316.18,316.76],[316.76,317.28],[317.28,317.94]]},{"el":"s5","text":"They describe the Taíno, whom he said were “none of them black.”","start":318.88,"end":322.6,"words":[[318.92,319.13],[319.13,319.68],[319.68,319.82],[319.82,320.3],[320.55,320.75],[320.75,320.89],[320.89,321.1],[321.1,321.24],[321.24,321.44],[321.44,321.58],[321.58,321.79],[321.79,322.14]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The first Africans documented in the Americas came with the Spanish: free Africans such as the conquistador Juan Garrido, and enslaved Africans, whom the Crown allowed to be shipped to Hispaniola from 1501.","start":322.95,"end":337.61,"words":[[323.0,323.15],[323.15,323.47],[323.47,324.11],[324.11,324.98],[324.98,325.14],[325.14,325.3],[325.3,325.93],[325.93,326.25],[326.25,326.49],[326.49,326.65],[326.65,327.2],[327.48,327.72],[327.72,328.35],[328.35,328.67],[328.67,328.83],[328.83,328.99],[328.99,329.78],[329.78,330.1],[330.1,330.73],[331.01,331.25],[331.25,331.89],[331.89,332.6],[332.88,333.12],[333.12,333.28],[333.28,333.67],[333.67,334.07],[334.07,334.23],[334.23,334.39],[334.39,334.7],[334.7,334.86],[334.86,335.74],[335.74,336.05],[336.05,337.01]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Enslaved Africans led one of the first revolts in the Americas there in 1521.","start":337.96,"end":343.72,"words":[[338.0,338.59],[338.59,339.19],[339.19,339.41],[339.41,339.63],[339.63,339.78],[339.78,339.93],[339.93,340.22],[340.22,340.82],[340.82,340.97],[340.97,341.11],[341.11,341.71],[341.71,341.93],[341.93,342.08],[342.08,343.27]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The idea that Africans came first grew partly as a response to centuries of racist claims that Africa had no history (the philosopher Hegel wrote in the 1830s that Africa was “no historical part of the world”).","start":344.07,"end":357.55,"words":[[344.11,344.26],[344.26,344.66],[344.66,344.89],[344.89,345.52],[345.52,345.83],[345.83,346.15],[346.15,346.38],[346.38,346.78],[346.78,346.93],[346.93,347.09],[347.09,347.64],[347.64,347.8],[347.8,348.5],[348.5,348.66],[348.66,349.21],[349.21,349.68],[349.68,349.92],[349.92,350.39],[350.39,350.62],[350.62,350.86],[350.86,351.41],[351.41,351.57],[351.57,352.2],[352.2,352.67],[352.67,352.98],[352.98,353.14],[353.14,353.29],[353.29,354.08],[354.08,354.32],[354.32,354.79],[354.79,355.02],[355.02,355.26],[355.26,356.04],[356.04,356.28],[356.28,356.44],[356.44,356.59],[356.59,356.98]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The documented record answers those claims on its own: the Mali Empire and Timbuktu's manuscripts, Great Zimbabwe (which colonial authorities falsely claimed others had built), and the Benin bronzes looted by British troops in 1897, some of which have now been returned.","start":357.9,"end":376.11,"words":[[357.95,358.1],[358.1,358.94],[358.94,359.32],[359.32,359.7],[359.7,360.0],[360.0,360.46],[360.46,360.61],[360.61,360.84],[360.84,361.15],[361.41,361.57],[361.57,361.87],[361.87,362.33],[362.33,362.56],[362.56,363.24],[363.24,364.23],[364.5,364.88],[364.88,365.49],[365.49,365.8],[365.8,366.56],[366.56,367.17],[367.17,367.62],[367.62,368.08],[368.08,368.39],[368.39,368.61],[368.61,369.0],[369.26,369.49],[369.49,369.64],[369.64,370.02],[370.02,370.56],[370.56,370.94],[370.94,371.17],[371.17,371.62],[371.62,372.01],[372.01,372.16],[372.16,373.45],[373.72,373.95],[373.95,374.1],[374.1,374.41],[374.41,374.63],[374.63,374.86],[374.86,375.09],[375.09,375.62]]},{"el":"h6","text":"Columbus Day and Indigenous Peoples' Day","start":377.01,"end":380.31,"words":[[377.05,377.81],[377.81,378.1],[378.1,378.39],[378.39,379.34],[379.34,379.91],[379.91,380.2]]},{"el":"s6","text":"President Benjamin Harrison proclaimed a one-time Columbus celebration in 1892, months after a mob in New Orleans lynched 11 Italian Americans; for Italian Americans facing discrimination, Columbus became a symbol of belonging.","start":381.01,"end":397.87,"words":[[381.05,381.73],[381.73,382.42],[382.42,382.95],[382.95,383.64],[383.64,383.79],[383.79,384.32],[384.32,384.93],[384.93,385.77],[385.77,385.92],[385.92,386.98],[387.25,387.63],[387.63,387.93],[387.93,388.09],[388.09,388.32],[388.32,388.47],[388.47,388.7],[388.7,389.15],[389.15,389.61],[389.61,390.06],[390.06,390.6],[390.6,391.36],[391.62,391.78],[391.78,392.31],[392.31,392.99],[392.99,393.45],[393.45,394.59],[394.86,395.47],[395.47,395.92],[395.92,396.07],[396.07,396.53],[396.53,396.68],[396.68,397.29]]},{"el":"s6","text":"It became a federal holiday in 1937.","start":398.22,"end":401.62,"words":[[398.27,398.4],[398.4,398.8],[398.8,398.93],[398.93,399.4],[399.4,399.87],[399.87,400.0],[400.0,401.14]]},{"el":"s6","text":"South Dakota renamed the day Native American Day in 1990, Berkeley adopted Indigenous Peoples' Day in 1992, and many states and cities have followed.","start":401.97,"end":413.49,"words":[[402.02,402.32],[402.32,402.86],[402.86,403.39],[403.39,403.55],[403.55,403.78],[403.78,404.23],[404.23,404.85],[404.85,405.07],[405.07,405.23],[405.23,406.3],[406.56,406.95],[406.95,407.48],[407.48,408.24],[408.24,408.7],[408.7,408.93],[408.93,409.08],[409.08,410.31],[410.57,410.8],[410.8,411.11],[411.11,411.57],[411.57,411.8],[411.8,412.18],[412.18,412.41],[412.41,412.94]]},{"el":"s6","text":"Presidential proclamations have changed with administrations.","start":413.84,"end":418.32,"words":[[413.88,414.67],[414.67,415.6],[415.6,415.82],[415.82,416.39],[416.39,416.61],[416.61,417.76]]}]}